3.8.text:
CHAPTER VIII

ASSISTANCE

Article 26

1. Assistance includes all integrated efforts to restore and strengthen the condition of victims, carried out by health workers, educators, police, prosecutors, courts, advocates, social workers, volunteer assistants and spiritual counselors.
2. Assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The duties of assistants are:
a. to provide sufficient information to victims about their rights;
b. to provide psychosocial services to victims so that victims feel safe and comfortable;
c. to accompany victims during the examination and medical recovery process;
d. to accompany victims during the medicolegal process;
e. to accompany victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
f. to monitor the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
g. to maintain the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by mass media;
h. to coordinate with other assistants; and
i. to provide continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 21

Forms of services for victims organized by UPTD PPA include:
a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services (visum);
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services;
e. economic independence services; and
f. spiritual services.

Article 29

1. Funding for protection of women and children victims of violence includes:
a. medical services, which include doctor's examination, action costs, hospital costs, medicine costs and medical support costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examination for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, consisting of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelter);
d. legal services;
e. economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
2. Funding management shall be carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is conducted transparently and can be legally accounted for.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 11

Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to appropriate, comfortable, and needs-based complaint handling;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. to receive continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 3

The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence are:
a. to prevent all forms of violence against women and children;
b. to provide protection and services to women and child victims of gender-based violence;
c. to provide a sense of security to women and child victims of violence;
d. to restore the physical, psychological and economic condition of women and child victims of violence;
e. the best interests of women and child victims of violence that occur in the domestic and/or public sphere; and
f. to take action against perpetrators of violence against women and children.

Article 12

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article ll, also receive special rights, as follows:
a. the right to survival, growth, and development;
b. the right to basic population services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 18

1. Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 17 is carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population and civil registration;
f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and micro enterprises;
i. mental and spiritual; and
j. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on the RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 19

1. In order to provide protection services to women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government shall establish UPTDPPA.
2. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to implement services for:
a. public complaints;
b. victim outreach;
c. case management;
d. temporary shelter;
e. mediation; and
f. victim assistance.
3. Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.
4. To assist in the protection of women and children, the Regional Government may establish:
a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Child-Friendly Regency Task Force or other designation;
d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
e. Majalengka Youth Children's Forum;
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM)GEMPITA.

